---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { SUPPORT_EMAIL } from '@golem/shared';

// WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. Apple can take money — a Stripe checkout, a customer portal, a
// signature-verified webhook, purchased Credits — and had not one written word about any of it.
// /docs/credits-and-limits covers the quota and its only money words are "no card". The FAQ has ten
// questions and none about a failed payment, an invoice or cancelling. So the first time a card was
// declined, the product's entire published position was an email address.
//
// EVERY CLAIM BELOW IS READ OFF apps/worker/src/billing.ts, not imagined, and
// tests/billing-help.test.mjs checks the two load-bearing ones against that file: that `past_due`
// still entitles (so "your plan keeps working while the card is retried" stays true), and that a
// lapsed period still drops to free. If someone tightens either rule, this page goes red naming the
// sentence that became a lie — which is the failure mode a billing page has, and the reason it is
// the most dangerous kind of documentation to leave behind.
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Billing & payments — Apple Docs"
  description="What happens when a payment fails, where invoices live, how cancelling works, and what to do when a payment went through but the plan has not changed."
  heading="Billing & payments"
>
  <p>
    Everything to do with money — cards, invoices, cancelling — lives in the billing portal, which
    is Stripe's, not ours. You reach it from <strong>Usage</strong> in the app
    (<a href="/app/usage">/app/usage</a>), from the button that reads
    <em>Manage billing, invoices and cancellation</em>. It appears once you are on a paid plan.
  </p>

  <h2 id="failed-payment">A payment failed or the card was declined</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Your plan keeps working.</strong> A failed renewal is usually an expired card, and we do
    not cut off a paying customer at the first retry. Stripe retries over the following days, and
    for as long as it is retrying you keep the allowance you are paying for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Update the card in the billing portal — that is the whole fix, and it applies immediately. If
    Stripe gives up before you do, the subscription ends and the account drops to Free: your
    projects, checkpoints and conversations are untouched, and the daily allowance becomes the free
    one. Nothing is deleted for non-payment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="invoices">Where invoices and receipts are</h2>
  <p>
    In the billing portal, under your payment history — every invoice, with a PDF, including ones
    from a subscription that has since ended. We do not hold a second copy of your billing history;
    the portal is the record.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you need a company name, address or VAT number on them, set it in the portal before the next
    invoice is issued. Stripe puts on an invoice what it knows at the moment it issues it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cancelling">Cancelling</h2>
  <p>
    Cancel in the billing portal. <strong>You keep the plan until the end of the period you have
    already paid for</strong> — cancelling on day 3 of a month does not take the allowance away on
    day 3. After that date the account is on Free, with everything still in it.
  </p>
  {/*
    "Credits you bought separately" presupposed a purchase a customer cannot make. The behaviour
    described is real — extra Credits sit in their own balance, survive a subscription ending, and
    splitSpend takes the day's allowance before touching them — but the only things that can put
    them there are the Stripe webhook and an admin route, and no code in this repository creates the
    payment-mode checkout the webhook is waiting for. The presupposition is removed and the gap is
    said out loud, because a reader planning around "I'll just buy more" needs to know now rather
    than at the moment their allowance ends.
  */}
  <p>
    Extra Credits, if an account has any, are separate from a subscription and do not expire with
    one. They stay on the account, and they are spent only after the day's included allowance is
    gone. <strong>There is no way to buy them today</strong> — no plan has a credit checkout, and
    <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a> says the same in its comparison table.
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-applied">I paid, and the plan still shows the old one</h2>
  <p>
    Give it a few seconds and press <em>Check again</em> on the Usage page. Plans are applied from
    Stripe's own webhook rather than from the page you land on after paying — deliberately, because
    a person who pays and closes the tab has still bought the thing, and a person who reaches the
    success page by typing the URL has not. The webhook usually arrives before you get back.
  </p>
  <p>
    If it is still wrong after a minute, that is worth reporting rather than paying again. Nothing
    you do on this page can double-charge you — but a second checkout could, so do not start one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="upgrading">Changing plan while already on a paid one</h2>
  <p>
    Moving between paid tiers happens in the portal, not through a new checkout, in both directions.
    A second checkout would <em>add</em> a subscription rather than replace the running one, and you
    would be billed for both. The app already routes you to the portal for this; it is only worth
    knowing if you were about to go looking for a purchase button.
  </p>

  <h2 id="charges">What is charged, and what is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing is charged before checkout completes.</strong> Starting one and closing it
      costs nothing, and the app says so where it happens.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Running out of Credits is not a charge.</strong> A request refused for lack of
      allowance is refused before it costs anything — see
      <a href="/docs/credits-and-limits">Credits &amp; limits</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A step that failed is not charged.</strong> Credits come from the compute a call
      actually reports, so a call that returned nothing reports nothing —
      <a href="/docs/troubleshooting#model-failures">Troubleshooting</a> covers what each failure
      means.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      A billing question a page cannot answer is worth an email:
      <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>. Include the email address on the
      account and roughly when the payment was — never a card number, which we neither need nor
      want, and which nobody at Apple can see.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
